key to rebuild discover.Rmd, which ships with its results already in place. -->

Before you download any camera data, it helps to know what the database holds: which
projects sampled your region, when, with what design, which species they recorded, and
whether their data are released yet. All of that lives in each project's metadata, which
is small and quick to fetch. This vignette searches WildObs, reads the metadata of what it
finds, shows where the same records are published in other standards, and ends with the
references to cite. It downloads no WildObs data tables.

```{r key}
library(WildObsR)

# your personal API key, read from .Renviron (see the Get started vignette)
api_key <- Sys.getenv("WILDOBSR_API_KEY")
```

## Search by place, time, species, design or person

`wildobs_mongo_query()` returns the IDs of projects that match every filter you give it.
A filter left out does not restrict the search, but at least one filter is needed:

| Filter | What it matches | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `spatial` | cameras inside a box, in decimal degrees | `list(xmin = 145, xmax = 154, ymin = -29, ymax = -10)` |
| `temporal` | surveys running at any time in a date range | `list(minDate = as.Date("2022-01-01"), maxDate = as.Date("2022-12-31"))` |
| `taxonomic` | projects that recorded any of these species | `c("Phascolarctos cinereus", "Felis catus")` |
| `samplingDesign` | the Camtrap DP sampling design | `"targeted"`, `"systematicRandom"`, `"opportunistic"`, ... |
| `contributors` | a person's name, email or ORCID, in any role | `"Zachary Amir"` |

A box over the whole continent lists every project a key can see:

```{r all}
# a box over Australia and its islands
australia <- list(xmin = 110, xmax = 160, ymin = -45, ymax = -9)

# every project visible to a public key
all_ids <- wildobs_mongo_query(api_key = api_key, spatial = australia)
length(all_ids)

# and those whose data tables can be downloaded today
open_ids <- wildobs_mongo_query(api_key = api_key, spatial = australia,
                                tabularSharingPreference = "open")
length(open_ids)
```

The difference is the **partial** projects. Their metadata are public, but their tables
are withheld, usually under an embargo that will end. They are worth knowing about: a
partial project in your study region is a dataset you could request from its contributors
or plan to use once it is released.

Filters combine as an intersection. For example, the projects that recorded feral cats in
2022 with a targeted design:

```{r combined}
# feral cats, surveyed in 2022, by a targeted design
cat_ids <- wildobs_mongo_query(
  api_key = api_key,
  temporal = list(minDate = as.Date("2022-01-01"), maxDate = as.Date("2022-12-31")),
  taxonomic = "Felis catus",
  samplingDesign = "targeted"
)
cat_ids
```

## Download metadata only

`metadata_only = TRUE` downloads each project's descriptor without its tables, which takes
seconds even for every project in the database. Partial projects always download this way.

```{r metadata}
# the descriptors of every visible project
meta <- wildobs_dp_download(api_key = api_key, project_ids = all_ids, metadata_only = TRUE)
```

## Read the metadata as tables

`extract_metadata()` turns one element of the metadata, across all packages, into a table
with a `DPID` column naming the project. Ask for several elements at once to get a named
list of tables.

```{r tables}
# design and data-sharing preference, one row per project
project <- extract_metadata(meta, "project")
sharing <- extract_metadata(meta, "WildObsMetadata")

# how the projects were designed
table(project$samplingDesign)

# and how they share their data
table(sharing$tabularSharingPreference)
```

The temporal metadata hold one row per survey (a `deploymentGroup`), so they show how long
each project has been running:

```{r temporal}
# start and end of every survey in every project
temporal <- extract_metadata(meta, "temporal")

# the span of each project, in years
span <- tapply(seq_len(nrow(temporal)), temporal$DPID, function(i) {
  # from the first survey's start to the last survey's end
  as.numeric(difftime(max(as.Date(temporal$end[i])), min(as.Date(temporal$start[i])),
                      units = "days")) / 365.25
}) # end per project
summary(span)
```

The spatial metadata give a bounding box for each landscape a project sampled. Plotted
together they show where WildObs has cameras:

```{r map, fig.alt = "Map of Australia with a point at the centre of each landscape sampled by a WildObs project, coloured by whether its data tables are open or partial."}
library(ggplot2)

# one bounding box per locationName per project
spatial <- extract_metadata(meta, "spatial")
# the centre of each box, since most landscapes are too small to see as boxes
spatial$x <- (spatial$xmin + spatial$xmax) / 2
spatial$y <- (spatial$ymin + spatial$ymax) / 2
# each landscape's data-sharing preference, from its project
spatial <- merge(spatial, sharing[, c("DPID", "tabularSharingPreference")], by = "DPID")

# grab an outline of Australia's states, as an sf object
aus <- sf::st_as_sf(ozmaps::ozmap_states)

ggplot() +
  # the states, in pale grey
  geom_sf(data = aus, fill = "grey95", colour = "grey60") +
  # the larger landscapes' boxes
  geom_rect(data = spatial, aes(xmin = xmin, xmax = xmax, ymin = ymin, ymax = ymax,
                                colour = tabularSharingPreference), fill = NA) +
  # a point at the centre of every landscape
  geom_point(data = spatial, aes(x = x, y = y, fill = tabularSharingPreference),
             shape = 21, colour = "white", size = 2.5) +
  # the same two colours for boxes and points
  scale_fill_manual(values = c(open = "darkgreen", partial = "darkorange"),
                    aesthetics = c("colour", "fill"), name = "Data tables") +
  # crop to the mainland and Tasmania
  coord_sf(xlim = c(113, 154), ylim = c(-44, -10)) +
  # count the landscapes and projects in the title
  labs(title = sprintf("%d landscapes across %d WildObs projects",
                       nrow(spatial), length(unique(spatial$DPID)))) +
  # no axes or grid, with the legend inside the empty ocean to the west
  theme_void() +
  theme(legend.position = "inside", legend.position.inside = c(0.12, 0.2))
```

## Which species were recorded

The taxonomic metadata list every taxon each project detected, with its rank and English
name, so you can tell which projects hold a species before downloading anything.

```{r taxa}
# every taxon each project recorded
taxa <- extract_metadata(meta, "taxonomic")

# taxa named to species, by their two-part scientific name
species <- taxa[taxa$taxonRank == "species" & grepl("^[A-Z][a-z]+ [a-z-]+$", taxa$scientificName), ]
# the number of projects that recorded each species, most widespread first
head(sort(table(species$scientificName), decreasing = TRUE), 10)
```

## Find the publications behind a project

Papers and datasets linked to a project are listed in its `relatedIdentifiers`, as DOIs.
WildObs adds one more type, the project's [RAiD](https://raid.org), a persistent
identifier for the research activity itself.

```{r related}
# links from each project to papers, datasets and its RAiD
related <- extract_metadata(meta, "relatedIdentifiers")
table(related$relatedIdentifierType, related$relationType)
```

## Find the contributors associated with a project

Each project lists the people behind it, with their role, organisation and
[ORCID](https://orcid.org). For a partial project, they are who to ask about access before
its embargo ends. `extract_metadata()` returns them as a table with one row per person per
project:

```{r contributors}
# everyone associated with each project, one row per person per project
collab <- extract_metadata(meta, "contributors")
# the roles people hold across all projects
table(collab$role)

# the partial projects, and how many months their tables are embargoed for
partial <- sharing[sharing$tabularSharingPreference == "partial",
                   c("DPID", "embargoPeriodMonths")]

# the principal investigators of the partial projects
partial_pi <- merge(partial, collab[collab$role == "principalInvestigator", ], by = "DPID")
# their names, organisations and embargo lengths
head(partial_pi[, c("DPID", "title", "organization", "embargoPeriodMonths")], 5)
```

The `email` column holds each contributor's address, and the `path` column their ORCID.
Write to the principal investigator, or to the person with the `contact` role where a
project names one. For a project with no address listed, write to
[data@wildobs.org.au](mailto:data@wildobs.org.au).

## Find WildObs data in other portals

WildObs also publishes the animal records of open projects to two national portals, so
that people who work in other standards can use them without this package:

| Portal | Standard | What it holds | Find it with |
|---|---|---|---|
| [Atlas of Living Australia](https://www.ala.org.au) (ALA) | Darwin Core occurrences, passed on to [GBIF](https://www.gbif.org) | one record per animal observation | the [`galah`](https://galah.ala.org.au) package |
| [TERN EcoPlots](https://ecoplots.tern.org.au) | the [TERN ontology](https://linkeddata.tern.org.au) for plot data | one record per animal observation, grouped by landscape | the EcoPlots API, or the Python library [`terndata.ecoplots`](https://github.com/ternaustralia/terndata.ecoplots) |

The records are the same in all three places. What the portals leave out are the camera
deployments and their dates of operation, so they tell you where and when a species was
recorded, but not how hard anyone looked. For occupancy or abundance models, which need
that sampling effort, download the data package from WildObs.

The checks below compare each portal with the two projects bundled with WildObsR, which
`example_datapackages()` loads without a download.

```{r bundled}
# the bundled copies of two open projects
bundled <- example_datapackages()

# the animal records of each, read from its observations table
animals <- lapply(bundled, function(dp) {
  # every observation in the package
  obs <- frictionless::read_resource(dp, "observations")
  # keep only the records of animals, which are what the portals receive
  obs[obs$observationType == "animal", ]
}) # end per project
```

### Atlas of Living Australia

In the ALA, WildObs is a data provider, "Wildlife Observatory of Australia", and each
WildObs project is a dataset, which the ALA calls a data resource. Searching the
providers finds WildObs, and counting its records by data resource lists every project
it has published:

```{r ala-provider, message = FALSE}
library(galah)

# query the Atlas of Living Australia
galah_config(atlas = "Australia", verbose = FALSE)

# WildObs as a data provider, with its ALA identifier
search_all(providers, "Wildlife Observatory")

# the number of records in each WildObs dataset in the ALA
ala_datasets <- galah_call() |>
  # only records that WildObs provided
  galah_filter(dataProviderUid == "dp5214") |>
  # one row per dataset, by its ALA identifier
  galah_group_by(dataResourceUid) |>
  # count records rather than download them
  atlas_counts()
ala_datasets
```

Each dataset's description in the ALA's collections registry gives its title, and
stores the WildObs project ID as its `guid`, which links the two:

```{r ala-guid}
# the ALA's description of each WildObs dataset, read as JSON
registry <- lapply(ala_datasets$dataResourceUid, function(dr) {
  jsonlite::fromJSON(paste0("https://collections.ala.org.au/ws/dataResource/", dr))
}) # end per dataset

# the WildObs project ID each dataset was published from
ala_datasets$WildObsID <- vapply(registry, function(reg) reg$guid, character(1))
# and its DOI at GBIF
ala_datasets$doi <- vapply(registry, function(reg) reg$doi, character(1))
as.data.frame(ala_datasets)
```

The Woomargama project is in the ALA as one of these datasets. Its records per species
match the bundled copy exactly:

```{r ala-compare}
# the ALA identifier of the Woomargama project
woomargama <- "NSW_Woomargama_wombats_Linley_2021-22_WildObsID_0010"
dr_uid <- ala_datasets$dataResourceUid[ala_datasets$WildObsID == woomargama]

# records per species in the ALA, counted without a download
ala_species <- galah_call() |>
  # only the Woomargama dataset
  galah_filter(dataResourceUid == dr_uid) |>
  # one row per scientific name
  galah_group_by(scientificName) |>
  atlas_counts()

# records per species in the bundled WildObs package
wildobs_species <- as.data.frame(table(scientificName = animals[[woomargama]]$scientificName),
                                 responseName = "wildobs")

# side by side, most recorded first
both <- merge(ala_species, wildobs_species, by = "scientificName", all = TRUE)
head(both[order(-both$count), ], 8)

# species where the two disagree
sum(both$count != both$wildobs, na.rm = TRUE)
```

Counts need no account, but downloading the records themselves does: the ALA asks for
an email address registered with it. With one stored in `.Renviron` as `ALA_EMAIL`, the
records of a dataset come down as one table:

```{r ala-download, eval = nzchar(Sys.getenv("ALA_EMAIL"))}
# the email address registered with the ALA, read from .Renviron
galah_config(email = Sys.getenv("ALA_EMAIL"))

# every occurrence record in the Woomargama dataset
occs <- galah_call() |>
  # only the relevant dataset
  galah_filter(dataResourceUid == dr_uid) |>
  # download the occurrences
  atlas_occurrences()
nrow(occs)
```

The ALA passes the records on to GBIF, which gave each WildObs dataset the DOI listed above.

### TERN EcoPlots

[EcoPlots](https://ecoplots.tern.org.au) is TERN's portal for plot-based ecological
data, where WildObs is one of the datasets. Its API is open: searching it needs no
account or key. Every search is a POST request whose body holds a `query`, and the
`discovery` endpoints list the values a query can use.

```{r tern-datasets}
# the EcoPlots API
ecoplots <- "https://ecoplots.tern.org.au/api/v1.0"

# list the values of one search field, optionally within an existing query
ecoplots_discover <- function(field, query = setNames(list(), character(0))) {
  # the query as a JSON body, keeping an empty query as {}
  body <- jsonlite::toJSON(list(query = query), auto_unbox = TRUE)
  # send it to the discovery endpoint for this field
  res <- httr::POST(paste0(ecoplots, "/discovery/", field), body = body,
                    httr::content_type_json())
  # stop if the request failed
  httr::stop_for_status(res)
  # read the answer as a table of names (key) and identifiers (uri)
  jsonlite::fromJSON(httr::content(res, "text", encoding = "UTF-8"))
} # end ecoplots_discover

# every dataset in EcoPlots
datasets <- ecoplots_discover("dataset")
datasets

# the identifier of the WildObs dataset
wildobs_uri <- datasets$uri[datasets$key == "WildObs"]
```

In EcoPlots, a site is one landscape (a WildObs `locationName`), and its name ends with
the WildObs project ID. Listing the sites therefore shows which projects are published:

```{r tern-sites}
# the WildObs query: everything from the WildObs dataset
wildobs_query <- list(dataset = list(wildobs_uri))

# every WildObs site in EcoPlots
sites <- ecoplots_discover("site_id", wildobs_query)
sites$key

# the type of record they hold, which a data request must name
feature <- ecoplots_discover("feature_type", wildobs_query)
feature$key
```

The `data` endpoint returns the records that match a query, here as CSV. One landscape
from the Wet Tropics project:

```{r tern-data}
# the Daintree landscape of the Wet Tropics project, and its animal records
daintree_query <- c(wildobs_query, list(
  site_id = list(sites$uri[sites$key == "Daintree_NP-wildobsid_0001"]),
  feature_type = list(feature$uri)
))

# ask for the records as CSV
res <- httr::POST(paste0(ecoplots, "/data/stream"), query = list(dformat = "csv"),
                  body = jsonlite::toJSON(list(query = daintree_query), auto_unbox = TRUE),
                  httr::content_type_json(), httr::add_headers(Accept = "text/event-stream"),
                  httr::timeout(120))
httr::stop_for_status(res)
# read the CSV into a table
tern <- utils::read.csv(text = httr::content(res, "text", encoding = "UTF-8"))

# one row per animal record
nrow(tern)
head(tern[, c("siteName", "scientificName", "animalCount_number", "animalCountResultTime")])
```

The same landscape in the bundled package holds the same records:

```{r tern-compare}
# the Wet Tropics project, and the cameras in its Daintree landscape
wet_tropics <- "ZAmir_QLD_Wet_Tropics_2022_WildObsID_0001"
deps <- frictionless::read_resource(bundled[[wet_tropics]], "deployments")
daintree_deps <- deps$deploymentID[deps$locationName == "Daintree_NP"]

# the animal records from those cameras
daintree <- animals[[wet_tropics]][animals[[wet_tropics]]$deploymentID %in% daintree_deps, ]

# the number of records, and of animals counted, in each source
c(ecoplots = nrow(tern), wildobs = nrow(daintree))
c(ecoplots = sum(tern$animalCount_number), wildobs = sum(daintree$count))
```

EcoPlots places every record at the landscape's single coordinate and gives its date
without a time, so it suits questions about where and when species occur rather than
camera-level analyses. TERN's [guide to the EcoPlots API in
R](https://ternaus.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TERNSup/pages/3042344973/How+to+Access+Data+from+an+API+using+R+-+EcoPlots)
describes more of the query fields.

## Cite what you use

Each project carries the citation its contributors asked for. `cite_datapackages()`
returns it, with the version of the database the data came from, as text, as BibTeX for a
reference manager, or as a table:

```{r cite}
# the two open projects bundled with WildObsR, as an example
examples <- c("ZAmir_QLD_Wet_Tropics_2022_WildObsID_0001",
              "NSW_Woomargama_wombats_Linley_2021-22_WildObsID_0010")

# formatted references, with the date the data were accessed
writeLines(cite_datapackages(meta[examples], accessed = Sys.Date()))

# or BibTeX
utils::toBibtex(cite_datapackages(meta[examples], format = "bibtex"))
```

Packages downloaded with their tables can also cite the products behind the WildObs
covariates, such as the Forest Landscape Integrity Index, with `covariates = TRUE`.

